ABS = {
"conservation_law_mirage_reactions":
"Mass conservation is chemistry's most basic law: a valid reaction has identical element counts on both sides. Data-driven reaction models are known to hallucinate unbalanced reactions, and the field fixes this by hard-coding balance into the architecture—never asking whether a standard model represents conservation internally. We surface a phenomenon we call the conservation mirage and make three typed claims. (C1) An autoregressive reaction transformer emits mostly balanced reactions via a shallow atom-copying heuristic, not a genuine internal law: steering an interpretable feature to change a product's chemistry (add a methyl, swap a halogen, remove an oxygen) reliably changes the chemistry but silently breaks atom balance, invisible to SMILES-validity checks and evident only from element counting. (C2) Conservation is linearly decodable from activations yet, under every intervention family we test, has no robust steerable direction the way local chemistry features do—a decodable-but-weakly-steerable global invariant. (C3) A cheap intervention-free balance-consistency signal flags mirage reactions better than validity or likelihood. Reactions are the ideal testbed because balance is an exact, free oracle (element-count vectors and the stoichiometry null space). We quantify how often validity-only screening would pass unbalanced outputs and translate it into wasted effort for retrosynthesis filtering, reaction editing, and autonomous-discovery loops. Either outcome is actionable: a real mirage changes how steered reactions are screened; a null certifies steering as conservation-safe. We release an open balance-audit benchmark and detector, and test the broader lesson that a global invariant can be non-steerable even where local properties are steerable.",

"kinetic_thermodynamic_steering_gap":
"A molecule's usefulness often depends on kinetics, not just thermodynamics: a metastable polymorph, a slow-off-rate binder, a fast-folding protein, or a low-barrier catalyst are defined by PATHWAYS and BARRIERS, not equilibrium endpoints. Generative models for molecules and proteins are trained almost entirely on static endpoints, yet interpretability-guided design increasingly steers their features to hit target properties. Our one primary, falsifiable claim is an endpoint-vs-pathway controllability gap: interpretable features causally control THERMODYNAMIC properties but not KINETIC ones, so steering toward a kinetic target silently shifts the thermodynamic ENDPOINT instead—a 'kinetics blind spot' invisible to equilibrium and validity checks. The strongest so-what: endpoint checks can certify a design that is kinetically invalid. Recent work shows a physics model trained on trajectories has steerable dynamical directions; we test the complementary hypothesis that generators trained WITHOUT dynamics lack a causal kinetic handle. Using ESM-2 protein LMs and a SMILES chemical LM with sparse autoencoders, plus cheap kinetic oracles (contact-order folding-rate proxies, ML-interatomic-potential barrier estimates, Langevin mean-first-passage-time) validated on a small higher-fidelity subset, we LOCATE thermodynamic and kinetic features, CAUSALLY contrast endpoint against pathway control with endpoint-matched pairs and randomized-label controls, quantify the blind spot, and AUDIT whether an internal kinetic-consistency signal flags silently-uncontrolled designs before expensive simulation. Concrete uses: slow-off-rate binders, folding-rate design, barrier-aware molecule generation. Either way is a general evaluation principle for generative design: a positive kinetic handle is a surprising capability; a null tells designers to add pathway-aware checks and not trust endpoint-only steering.",

"epistasis_steering_blindspot":
"A protein's function is shaped not only by which residue sits at each position but by epistasis: the coupling that makes the best residue at one position depend on another, underlying compensatory mutations, allosteric linkages, and folding cooperativity. Interpretability-guided design steers language-model features to install properties, and pLMs are known to internally capture epistatic couplings—yet every demonstrated steering target is an additive, single-position property. No prior work has tested causal steering of epistatic-coupling features in pLM SAEs. We ask a minimal, defensible question: can interpretable features causally control epistasis, or only additive traits? Our one primary hypothesis, with a single fallback, is that additive control succeeds while coupling control is at best weakly steerable—an 'epistasis blind spot': steering to install a target coupling (a compensatory pair, a contact-based or long-range functional linkage) reliably shifts additive properties while the intended coupling fails to form, invisible to single-mutant checks but exposed by a double-mutant-cycle oracle. Epistasis is uniquely suited because it is exactly quantifiable (epsilon = ddG_AB - ddG_A - ddG_B) from combinatorial deep-mutational-scanning data. Using ESM-2 with TopK and Ordered sparse autoencoders, we LOCATE additive and coupling features, CAUSALLY contrast their steerability, quantify the blind spot, and AUDIT whether an internal coupling-consistency signal flags silently-uncoupled designs. The concrete workflow change if the blind spot holds: engineers steering pLMs should audit couplings with double-mutant-cycle-style checks before synthesis. Either result is publishable—a steerable coupling handle would directly enable compensatory/allosteric design; a clean negative, replicated across models and oracles, delimits what feature-steering can control.",

"magnitude_dial_blindspot":
"Real scientific design specifies numbers, not directions: a chemist needs logP = 2.5, an enzyme engineer needs 40% helix content, not merely 'more'. Interpretability-guided design steers language-model features to hit properties, but every demonstrated result reports only that the property increased or appeared. Our single falsifiable claim: scientific design features are presence-steerable but not set-point calibrated—steering the SAME property along the SAME direction moves it monotonically yet cannot dial it to a specified numeric target, and control degrades sharply at high magnitudes. We name this the magnitude blind spot and define it operationally before any experiment. Borrowing a well-established cognitive-science result—that language models encode numeric magnitude in a logarithmically-compressed, causally-manipulable subspace—we test whether scientific generators inherit this compression and whether it undermines quantitative control. Using ESM-2 protein LMs and a SMILES chemical LM with sparse autoencoders and exact continuous oracles (RDKit logP/MW/TPSA; DSSP helix/sheet; GRAVY), we LOCATE presence features and magnitude directions; CAUSALLY test presence-steerability vs calibrated set-point steerability, measuring set-point error and reporting calibration and error-vs-target curves; INTERPRET whether the code is log-compressed and whether a log-reparameterized dial—evaluated separately on held-out targets, so the fix is not confused with the finding—restores precision; and AUDIT whether an internal magnitude-consistency signal flags off-target designs. Exact numeric targets matter for formulation logP windows and secondary-structure quotas. Either way changes evaluation: presence-only steering must report set-point error, and a calibrated dial would be a usable quantitative design knob.",

"convergence_order_rigidity":
"Iterative generative models—across images, audio, and language—lock in attributes in a strikingly consistent coarse-to-fine order: global structure before fine detail. We run the first causal test of whether this convergence ORDER is rigid or reorderable in scientific diffusion generators of proteins and molecules. This matters because real design often wants to prioritize a fine property (a motif, a functional group) while a coarse prerequisite (fold topology, scaffold) is still forming, and it is unknown whether a frozen model can honor such a request or will silently fail. Unlike coarse-to-fine molecular diffusion models that are ARCHITECTED to be hierarchical, we study EMERGENT order in frozen generators. We separate three contributions: measuring the property convergence order, a causal reordering test, and an order-violation alarm. We hypothesize order rigidity: interpretable-feature interventions shift a property's timing WITHIN its natural slot, but REORDERING—forcing a fine property to converge before its coarse prerequisite—yields objects that pass fluency/validity spot-checks yet are structurally broken, indicating a model-internal (not merely stylistic) coarse-to-fine dependency. Using protein and molecular diffusion generators with sparse autoencoders on per-timestep denoiser activations, we MEASURE convergence order against hard oracles, CAUSALLY test reordering versus in-slot timing shifts with strength sweeps and null controls, test whether coarse features gate fine ones, and AUDIT whether an intervention-free order-violation signal flags silently-broken designs for early abort during expensive sampling. Either way is informative: rigidity tells wet-lab pipelines not to request fine properties before coarse prerequisites; free reorderability would overturn the cross-modal coarse-to-fine dogma for scientific generators.",

"cell_identity_vs_response_steering":
"Single-cell foundation models (scFMs) are promoted for in-silico perturbation screening—predicting how a cell's transcriptome shifts under a gene knockdown or activation—a task that could accelerate drug and target discovery. Interpretability work shows sparse-autoencoder (SAE) features of scFMs are interpretable and steerable, but every demonstrated target is a categorical IDENTITY concept; separately, benchmarks find scFMs capture co-expression rather than causal regulatory signal. We unite these threads with four typed contributions: (1) a contrastive test of identity- vs response-steerability; (2) a response-blind-spot phenomenon—steering toward a target perturbation response reliably shifts cell IDENTITY while failing to reproduce the correct differentially-expressed-gene (DEG) direction; (3) a signed-DEG oracle built from held-out Perturb-seq ground truth; and (4) an internal response-consistency audit. We define a 'response feature' minimally as one whose steering moves the signed-DEG direction beyond what its identity shift alone explains. Using scGPT/Geneformer with SAEs, we LOCATE identity and response features, CAUSALLY contrast their controllability (does the steered cell's DEG signature match the true perturbation's direction and specificity?), interpret whether response units are genuine or identity proxies, and audit reliability. The concrete stake: identity-steering that masquerades as response control produces costly false positives in target discovery. Either result matters—a working response handle would be a surprising, high-value capability; a clean negative, replicated across models, datasets, and intervention types, tells the field to stop reporting identity-program success as evidence of causal perturbation control and to screen with a directional DEG oracle before wet-lab validation.",

"evolutionary_time_steering":
"A protein's sequence carries not just what it does but WHEN in evolutionary history it existed: ancestral sequence reconstruction (ASR) is foundational in evolutionary biology and a promising route to robust enzymes. Protein language models are known to encode phylogeny as a decodable geometric manifold, and generative models are increasingly proposed for ASR—yet whether this encoded structure is a CAUSALLY STEERABLE design lever is unknown, and prior latent-interpolation ASR fails despite the latent containing phylogenetic signal. We run the first causal steering test of evolutionary DEPTH in a protein LM. Our single primary hypothesis is an evolutionary-depth blind spot with a single primary metric (placement-depth error): steering an interpretable feature to move a sequence 'back in time' reliably preserves protein-family identity while failing to place it at the intended depth or match the substitution/indel statistics of true ancestors—invisible to family-membership and validity checks but exposed by a phylogenetic-placement oracle. Using ESM-2 (and optionally Evo 2 for DNA) with sparse autoencoders, we LOCATE family-identity and depth features, CAUSALLY contrast their steerability against gold-standard maximum-likelihood ASR ancestors and tree placement, interpret whether depth units are genuine or shallow proxies, and audit whether an internal phylogenetic-consistency signal flags misplaced designs. Silent misplacement wastes resurrection experiments and misleads biological inference. Either way is actionable: a steerable evolutionary-time handle would be a striking advance for interpretable ASR and ancestral-protein engineering; a decodable-but-unsteerable result tells ASR practitioners that reading phylogeny from a pLM does not license generating ancestors by feature steering.",
}

